# astro-module.scope — which directories of this repository are `//astro`.
#
# The repository root IS the module: gclient places this repository at
# `chromium/src/astro`, so `browser/` is `//astro/browser`. But the root also
# still holds the pre-module tree (`src/`, `webui/`, `patches/`) that #8 is
# retiring, and those files are NOT in the build graph — `BUILD.gn` says so
# outright. Something has to answer "which of these directories is the module"
# before `checkdeps` can be pointed at it, and inferring the answer from what
# happens to be on disk is how a scan silently stops covering a layer.
#
# tools/check-module-layering.sh reads this file and runs Chromium's
# `buildtools/checkdeps/checkdeps.py` over each declared layer.
#
# Format:  <kind> <top-level-directory> [key=value ...]
#
#   layer     Part of `//astro`. checkdeps scans it against the include_rules
#             its DEPS chain declares, and EVERY source file git tracks under
#             it must turn up in what checkdeps considered — a layer that goes
#             unscanned is a failure, not a pass.
#
#   excluded  In the repository, not in the module. Requires owner= naming the
#             issue that removes it, because this is a temporary arrangement:
#             an excluded directory is source code nothing checks. When the
#             directory goes, its line here must go with it — a declaration
#             that no longer matches the tree fails the gate in both
#             directions.
#
# Every OTHER top-level directory is checked to hold no C++, Java or proto
# source at all. That is what makes the two lists below safe to be short: a new
# directory carrying source is a failure until somebody declares which of the
# two it is, so nothing new can arrive unscanned.
#
# Comments and blank lines are ignored. Entries name one top-level directory
# each — no slashes, no "..".

# --- The module ---------------------------------------------------------------

# The bottom layer. Its DEPS subtracts `-chrome` and `-content/public/browser`,
# so it is the layer a stray `#include "chrome/..."` has to fail in for the
# layering to mean anything.
layer    common

# The one layer granted a `chrome/` header, and the reason the grant sits in
# browser/DEPS rather than at the module root: checkdeps inherits downward, so
# a root-level grant would hand the same permission to `common/` and to every
# layer Astro has not written yet.
layer    browser

# --- In the repository, not in the module --------------------------------------

# The pre-//astro overlay tree. It is copied into Chromium by
# tools/sync-overlay.sh rather than compiled as part of `//astro`, and it does
# not obey `//astro`'s include_rules: 39 of its files reach into `chrome/`,
# which is exactly what the overlay exists to do and exactly what the module
# forbids. Scanning it would report the old system as a defect in the new one.
excluded src  owner=8
